WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.760
It's six-ish on Sunday night. Welcome to the Sharon hour with me Sharon James on Laguna's one and only listener supported member supported

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:23.120
public FM radio station KX935. I have a very special interview tonight. An ex CIA agent. He was head of counterterrorism in Pakistan after 9-11.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:30.320
He has written a book called The Reluctant Spy, my secret life in the CIA's war on terror.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:39.360
His name is John Kiriaku. I met him in Los Angeles at the Penn Literary Gala where he won a special prize.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:46.080
He had to race back home, so my interview with him is over the telephone. He's back in Washington,

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.920
but I hope it's clear. He's very forthcoming. He's going to tell us about the inner workings

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:58.880
of the CIA along with a couple of James Bond style adventures during the course of his career there.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.880
Here then is my interview with John Kiriaku. I guess my first question is what inspired you to be a spy?

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:16.320
Well, I kind of went about it in an odd way in that I really didn't set out to be a spy.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.540
What happened was, it's actually kind of a funny story, I was in graduate school at

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.500
George Washington University and I had a professor who was teaching a class called

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.700
the Psychology of Leadership and this was a fascinating class, right? We were

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.020
learning about how psychology and in some cases mental illness played a role

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:43.340
in decision-making among world leaders through history. So as part of the

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:50.340
This professor assigned us a writing project, and what we had to do was to shadow our bosses

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:56.340
for a week and write a paper about them, not just a paper but a psychological analysis.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.340
So I was working for a labor union at the time, the United Food and Commercial Workers Union.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.340
In what capacity, John?

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:08.340
I was just an intern working for a polling unit that they had where they would take

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.780
where they would take public opinion polls for other unions,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.140
other ASL-CIO member unions.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.380
So this guy was a sociopath.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.500
He was violent.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.860
And I wrote a paper after it ran in with him,

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.540
saying that he was a sociopath,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.340
and that I believe he had psychopathic tendencies.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:25.580
And I gave some examples.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.220
So I turned in the paper, and a week later,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.180
I got it back, and I had gotten an A.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.020
But the professor wrote in the margin,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.620
he said, I've never done this before,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.580
but I urge you to quit this job.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.140
I agree with your assessment that he's a sociopath,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.180
and possibly a psychopath, and I think you should quit.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.660
Please see me after class.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.980
I went to the paper, and I went to see him after class,

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.860
and he sat me down in his office.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.060
And he said, look, I'm not really a professor here.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.860
I'm actually undercover as a professor.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.460
I'm a senior CIA officer, acting

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:00.660
at what's called a spotter, looking for graduate students

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.620
who I believe would be a good fit at the CIA.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.860
Would you be interested in working at the CIA?

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.860
Fantastic.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.820
I had never thought about it and I had no job and I had no prospects for a job and I was

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.180
going to graduate soon and get married right after that and I said sure I wouldn't mind

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:22.700
working for the CIA and he started the whole process, the testing and the background investigation

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.660
and the polygraph and the medical stuff and several months later I found myself in the

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:29.660
CIA.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:36.660
You're tuned to The Sharon Now with me Sharon James on Laguna's One and Only KX 93.5.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.660
My guest tonight is a fascinating man, John Kiriakou.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:48.660
He's ex-CIA, has written a book called The Reluctant Spy, My Secret Life in the CIA's War on Terror.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.660
He was head of counter-terrorism in Pakistan after 9-11.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:59.980
He started off in analysis, more of a disk job, found that ultimately not exciting enough,

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.940
and took the tests to become an agent in the field.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.540
I want to ask what those tests were actually.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:13.660
Well, there were two rounds of tests. The first round of tests was easy for me. It was three

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:20.860
fold. One, there was a very long multiple choice test on current events. And frankly,

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.900
it was so easy i remember telling friends that you don't even have to read the newspaper every day

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:30.780
if you just look at the headline or look at the front page every day without actually reading

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.860
anything you're still going to do well on this test and i even wrote in my book i remember

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:42.460
one of the questions was was the prime minister of grief is a andres papadreo he landed brezhnev

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:49.340
c maltsikong or d youth band i mean seriously even if you didn't know you could assume which

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.980
Which one was the Greek name and which was the Asian name?

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:53.380
Exactly, it was that easy.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.140
The second test in that round was, it was a large, fold-out map of the world, and all

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.700
the countries were blank, and we had to fill in the names of all the countries.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:07.020
I would have failed that immediately because my geography is terrible, so.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.020
God.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:12.940
Today the truth, countries change names all the time, the country gets carved out all

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:13.940
the time.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:14.940
It can be confusing.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.180
And then the third one was very long.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:21.220
was several thousand questions and they weren't even really questions as much as

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:26.900
they were statements and you had to say agree or disagree and I remembered that

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:33.260
one of them was I liked boxing and that the truth is I really didn't care about

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:39.540
boxing one way or the other but you have to say yes or no and I thought well

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.260
I suppose if I'm clicking through the channels and Mike Tyson's fighting

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:54.820
Texas is big at the time, I suppose I'll say yes, I think. But then like 285 questions later it says

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:59.940
I like boxing and I'm like oh no, what did I say the first time? I can't remember. I think I said

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:06.980
yes, I may have said no, and then another 415 questions later it says I like boxing. So I saw

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.900
what they were trying to do. It was a psychological exam and they just wanted to see

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:15.220
if you were consistent and if you were trying to tell them what you thought they wanted to hear.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:24.180
You're listening to the Sharon hour on Lagunas KX 93.5. I'm Sharon James. My guest tonight is

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:31.780
ex-CIA agent John Kiryaku telling us about when he first joined the CIA, recruited by a professor

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:37.940
at his college and given this incredibly lengthy written test. And I ended up getting a call

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.900
a couple of weeks later from somebody in personnel and they said that I had blown the doors off

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:49.540
these exams so I went on to round two and round two was I had to go to this nondescript building

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:57.060
in Virginia and when I arrived I was met by a psychologist a psychiatrist and an anthropologist

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.700
they were all sitting on one side of the table and I was sitting on the other and I sat down

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:06.420
and they asked me questions like can you describe your relationship with your mother

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:10.420
And was your father the disciplinarian of the family?

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:14.420
And one that really struck me was, have you ever betrayed a friendship?

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:18.420
And I, you know, what are the right answers to those questions? I had no idea.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:22.420
Then they told me that they needed to take some hair, blood, and urine,

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:26.420
and they sent me on my way, and then I got another call a couple weeks later saying

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:30.420
I had aced all those tests. And then I was sent to the CIA headquarters

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:34.420
for interviews. I did well in the interviews. And then ironically, I was hired

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:50.420
Now, have you ever betrayed a friendship? What did you answer?

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:54.420
I said, oh my gosh, no, I don't think so.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.420
I said, let me think about it for a minute.

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:59.420
And they said, no, no, that's the answer we were looking for.

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:07.000
Wow, I mean that's interesting because I would think being a spy the difficult thing is there are going to be lots of

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:11.460
Betrayals you have to have that capacity lots of betrayals because in operations

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:17.180
That's the nature of the job right then a season's case officer will tell you that those are not true

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:25.300
Friendship that you don't allow yourself to become truly friends with somebody you want that person to think that your friends

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:31.300
He wants that person to commit espionage for you, or in some cases treason for you.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:38.300
But that doesn't mean that you allow yourself, in your own heart, to really become friends with him.

00:08:38.300 --> 00:08:39.300
You see, that I would...

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.300
It's very cynical.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:41.300
Yeah.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.300
It sounds awful.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.300
It sounds awful.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.300
Well, here's why it sounds weird, and here's what goes through my head, that if you have

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:54.300
the capacity for duplicity, you would think that would span all areas of your life.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.260
You are able to compartmentalize, right?

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.900
Yeah, in many cases it does.

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:01.900
Now, a lot of CIA case officers find themselves

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:03.700
in legal trouble afterwards,

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.500
and not for things like whistleblowing,

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:08.660
but for things like fraud, for example.

00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:13.140
I actually served in prison with a former CIA officer

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.660
who was in prison on multiple counts of fraud,

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:19.420
and it's because you lie and trick and cheat and steal

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:22.580
for a living for 25 years, that's 30 years.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:27.220
get used to a certain lifestyle and a lot of guys are not able to turn that off. And

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:33.620
similarly, the divorce rate at the CIA is sky high, higher than any other governmental

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:39.300
organization. And it's because, you know, people are lying and, and cheating every day

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:42.980
all day, and then they don't know how to turn it off when they go home at night.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:47.060
And they end up lying to their wives and cheating on their wives until marriages fail.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:48.660
So how does one turn it off?

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:53.300
You have to have a strong constitution. You have to be able to turn it off.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:59.540
Now, in my case, I was able to turn it off, but my first wife never believed me when I

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:03.140
said that I was going out in the middle of the night because I had to work.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.420
She couldn't tell her anything more than the fact that I had to work,

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:12.980
and so she left me and my first marriage fell apart. My second wife was a CIA officer,

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.380
so when I told her I was going to work, she understood. She knew I was telling the truth

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:25.180
You're tuned to the Sharon hour on Lagunas KX 93.5.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:32.180
I'm Sharon James and my guest tonight is John Kiriakou, ex CIA agent, also head of counter

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:40.060
terrorism in Pakistan after 9-11, also ABC correspondent on terrorism issues.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:43.940
He was recruited into the CIA from college.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:46.700
He had many excellent qualifications

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.420
including speaking fluent Arabic and Greek.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:53.300
And then he took a very comprehensive test

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.220
that he's been telling us about.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:57.580
It included one particular question

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.100
that stuck in my head as we talked.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.380
I was wondering on the yes, no answers on your test.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:05.700
Do you think that that was

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:09.180
because they wanted you to be unequivocal as well?

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.340
Well, I think so.

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:11.700
I've said this a lot.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:17.220
The CIA likes to think that most issues are black and white, right or wrong, when it comes

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:19.900
to internal policies.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:23.700
When it comes to CIA operations, they like to tell you that everything is a shade of

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:24.700
gray.

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:28.420
And that's where I differed with the CIA's leadership because when it came to the issue

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:33.180
of torture, I believe that torture wasn't black and white, it was not a shade of gray.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:39.380
Now a CIA psychiatrist told me once that the CIA looks to hire people with sociopathic

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:49.380
not sociopaths. Sociopaths are unable to feel empathy, and they can pass polygraphs very easily, but they can't be trusted.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:56.380
But people with sociopathic tendencies are people who are comfortable working in moral and legal grey areas.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:12:04.380
So if you're ordered to fly to a foreign country and let's say break into a house, or break into an office and steal a file,

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:09.380
or recruit somebody to convince him to give you his country's secrets.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:14.880
You can't lay in bed at night and beat yourself up over the immorality of doing such a thing.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.380
You just have to do it.

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.380
And so that's the kind of person that they look for.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:25.380
But doing it for a cause, I presume, and which you think is not the sociopathic.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:27.380
We all believed we were the good guys.

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:30.380
And for me, that changed after September 11th, of course,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:31.880
and I've been quite public about that.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:33.880
But yeah, I always believed we were the good guys.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.320
So I had no trouble doing my job.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:43.360
Can you, can you ever empathize with your adversary thinking that they're thinking they're the

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:47.540
good guys or can you not let yourself go there?

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:54.720
You know, there are always at least two sides to a story, certainly, and sure, sure, you

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:59.960
can allow yourself to believe that others are the good guys too, but you're not asking

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:06.520
people to commit espionage for you for ideological purposes 99% of the time

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:10.840
you're usually asking to do it for money I mean there are a lot of different

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:16.220
motivators for people who end up working on a CIA payroll but most of the time

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:20.760
I'm gonna say 95% of the time it's money do it for money so that's like

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:26.520
basically mercenary right sure you deal with a lot of scumbags in this job now

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:31.720
there are others who will do it for ideology. You know, they love the United States and they want to

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:38.840
help. There are some who do it for revenge. Maybe they were passed over for a job and they want to

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:43.800
exact revenge on their employer or on their government. And they're starting to do it.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:48.520
You should have the thrill of doing it. I met one guy in particular who would take

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:54.200
ridiculous chances and he wouldn't take any money from me, but he did it just because

00:13:54.200 --> 00:14:00.200
he wrote this wrote billion it's interesting i hadn't really even thought about this financial aspect

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:06.440
of it it always that's really the most important part of the financial aspect if you are let's say

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:12.520
i'm just making this up if you're an iranian nuclear scientist right or a north korean nuclear

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:18.840
scientist and you're willing to allow yourself to be recruited by a cia officer and you have

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:25.320
access to a CIA officer because you're traveling overseas. Oh my god, the money, the money that

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:31.320
could come your way is more than you could ever imagine. Interesting. The mercenary aspect wasn't

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:37.240
something that immediately sprung to mind in terms of international espionage. I don't know about

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:44.440
you listener. You're tuned to The Sharon Hour with me Sharon James on Laguna's KX93.5.

