not realising that they had somebody who spoke both.

00:54:53.540 --> 00:54:57.540
When I volunteered, they gave me the job and I switched operations.

00:54:57.540 --> 00:55:02.540
And just going back to the tests, it sounds like you aced it because of your candor.

00:55:02.540 --> 00:55:06.540
So is candor anything that they look for in recruits?

00:55:06.540 --> 00:55:08.540
I think so, especially in operations.

00:55:08.540 --> 00:55:14.040
I've always been very candid. I find that to be the way to go, you know. Honesty is the best policy.

00:55:14.040 --> 00:55:20.540
And I think that they liked that, and I think that the test is very highly on the extrovert chart.

00:55:20.540 --> 00:55:27.040
And they thought that that, along with the recommendation from my professor, meant that I would be successful there.

00:55:27.040 --> 00:55:29.540
And indeed I was. I had a very good career.

00:55:29.540 --> 00:55:34.040
And for anyone wanting to go into it, do you have any words of advice?

00:55:34.040 --> 00:55:37.540
Yeah. Learn hard languages. Really hard ones.

00:55:37.540 --> 00:55:42.100
They're not taking people anymore who have, you know, French, German, Italian and Spanish.

00:55:42.100 --> 00:55:46.500
You need Arabic, Farsi, Dari, Urdu, Pashto,

00:55:46.500 --> 00:55:51.860
Sindhi, Punjabi, Tajik, Uzbek, Chinese, Korean and Russian.

00:55:51.860 --> 00:55:56.260
Wow. And that's the top, I mean, in India and in Pakistan,

00:55:56.260 --> 00:55:59.220
there are so many different languages, right? And very different.

00:55:59.860 --> 00:56:04.740
Yeah, there are dozens of languages in South Asia and we just haven't mastered them.

00:56:04.740 --> 00:56:09.300
and how do you know which language you're dealing with if you're interrogating somebody

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:12.340
and they're pretending not to understand, for instance?

00:56:12.340 --> 00:56:14.180
Well, you know, that's also a good question.

00:56:14.180 --> 00:56:18.420
Normally you know something about the person you're talking to.

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:21.140
If he's an Arab, we have lots of Arabic speakers.

00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:26.900
If he's Pakistani, you can borrow somebody from the Pakistani police or military to interrogate him.

00:56:26.900 --> 00:56:31.780
But there were a couple times in my experience where we would just kind of sit there and look

00:56:31.780 --> 00:56:39.460
likely each other. We captured six guys who looked vaguely Chinese or Asian or whatever,

00:56:39.460 --> 00:56:44.580
Central Asian, Northern Asian. I didn't know. None of us knew. And they didn't respond to

00:56:44.580 --> 00:56:48.820
Arabic. They didn't respond to Farsi. They didn't respond to any of the languages that we had in

00:56:48.820 --> 00:56:54.740
the station. And it turned out that they were readers. They were from Western China. They're

00:56:54.740 --> 00:56:59.620
Muslims from Western China. And they spoke their own unique language. They spoke a little bit

00:56:59.620 --> 00:57:05.620
of Chinese. And finally we were able to identify an officer who spoke Chinese. He came up to

00:57:05.620 --> 00:57:10.740
Pakistan to interview them and we didn't really want to keep them or hold them. We just kind

00:57:10.740 --> 00:57:15.540
of scooped them up. The Pakistanis didn't want them because they were there illegally. So we asked

00:57:15.540 --> 00:57:19.700
the Chinese, well, do you want them? The Chinese said no, we don't want them. And these poor

00:57:19.700 --> 00:57:23.940
bakers ended up going to Guantanamo. It's because no country wanted them.

00:57:23.940 --> 00:57:28.340
Which is another shocker of Guantanamo. We haven't even gotten into that, but gosh.

00:57:28.340 --> 00:57:32.580
Oh dear, well John, this has been absolutely wonderful. I could go on forever.

00:57:32.580 --> 00:57:35.140
Thank you for taking the time. It's been a delightful conversation.

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:39.300
And I thank you for this wonderful book. When you say reluctant spy,

00:57:39.300 --> 00:57:41.220
do you want to go into that quickly?

00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:45.140
Yeah, I was reluctant I suppose for a couple of reasons. I loved the job but

00:57:45.140 --> 00:57:48.420
I was reluctant to just watch my first marriage fall apart.

00:57:48.420 --> 00:57:53.460
I was certainly reluctant to allow myself to be included in the use of these

00:57:53.460 --> 00:57:57.380
so-called enhanced interrogation techniques. I refused, frankly.

00:57:57.380 --> 00:58:03.880
I was reluctant to leave, but it was the best for me and the best for my family, and I had a good career, but it was time to move on.

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:08.180
How do you live a normal life after having such as heightened one?

00:58:08.180 --> 00:58:12.180
It's hard to. I'm not sure that my wife and I will ever really have normal lives.

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:14.180
Now is your wife still in the CIA?

00:58:14.180 --> 00:58:16.680
No, she left the CIA in 2012.

00:58:16.680 --> 00:58:19.180
Was it difficult while she was in a new route?

00:58:19.180 --> 00:58:24.380
Yeah, it was frankly, and I still have a lot of friends in the CIA people I stay in touch with.

00:58:24.380 --> 00:58:27.880
I mostly missed the travel. The travel was truly incredible.

00:58:27.880 --> 00:58:30.880
I think a lot of people who go to war feel the same.

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:36.880
You know, a lot of soldiers, people in the military, much as it scared them and was awful.

00:58:36.880 --> 00:58:39.880
It was also, again, such a heightened experience.

00:58:39.880 --> 00:58:42.880
And a collegial one too. That's the biggest thing.

00:58:42.880 --> 00:58:44.880
Well, indeed it is.

00:58:44.880 --> 00:58:50.880
Well, I'm afraid we've come to the end of my sharing hour for tonight on Laguna's KX93.5.

00:58:50.880 --> 00:58:58.400
I'm Sharon James, and you can re-listen to this show on podcast also at kx935.com

00:58:58.400 --> 00:59:04.800
slash shows slash The Sharon Are, that's Sharing Without The G, our With The H.

00:59:04.800 --> 00:59:08.560
My guest John Kayaku's book is The Reluctant Spy,

00:59:08.560 --> 00:59:12.880
My Secret Life in the CIA's War on Terror, and I will join you again

00:59:12.880 --> 00:59:15.760
unterrified next Sunday night at 6.

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:52.880
In the redwood forest, in the Gulf stream waters, this land was made for you and me.

